\section{Abstract}

CloudGuard is a proposed web-based tool for identifying common security misconfigurations in Amazon Web Services (AWS) Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies, IAM role trust policies, and S3 bucket policies. A user will upload policy JSON files or select predefined sample configurations. A rule-based engine will detect risky permission patterns, assign a severity level, explain the supporting evidence, and recommend changes that follow the principle of least privilege. Where the supplied configuration contains identity, role, and resource relationships, CloudGuard will build a graph and show plausible access paths. The first version will analyze sample configurations offline and will not connect to a live AWS account. Detection performance will be measured on a labeled set of secure policies, intentionally misconfigured policies, and invalid JSON inputs.

\section{Introduction}

Cloud computing platforms such as Amazon Web Services provide scalable infrastructure for storing data and deploying applications. Improperly configured permissions, excessive privileges, insecure trust relationships, and publicly accessible storage can introduce significant security risks.

AWS Identity and Access Management controls who can access cloud resources and which actions they are allowed to perform. Incorrectly configured IAM policies can violate the principle of least privilege and expose sensitive resources to unauthorized access.

CloudGuard is a proposed web-based security analysis tool for common misconfigurations in AWS policy configurations. The system will analyze uploaded JSON policy files, identify potentially risky permissions, assess their severity, visualize possible attack paths, and recommend remediation. The initial implementation will operate on sample AWS configurations and will not require access to a live AWS account.

\subsection{Problem Statement}

Cloud environments become vulnerable when IAM policies grant excessive permissions, trust policies allow unintended role assumption, or S3 bucket policies permit unauthorized public access. These misconfigurations are difficult to identify by manual inspection when permissions and trust relationships are spread across multiple identities and resources.

Finding one misconfiguration does not, by itself, show how that misconfiguration contributes to a broader security risk. CloudGuard will automate detection of selected configuration weaknesses and explain how connected permissions can create potential paths to sensitive resources. The approach combines policy analysis, risk assessment, graph-based attack-path simulation, and security recommendations.

\section{Goals and Objectives}

The objectives of CloudGuard are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item To develop a web-based application that accepts and analyzes AWS IAM policy JSON files.
    \item To detect potentially risky permission patterns, including wildcard actions, wildcard resources, and excessive privileges.
    \item To analyze IAM role trust policies and identify potentially unsafe role-assumption configurations.
    \item To examine S3 bucket policies for configurations that may allow unauthorized public access.
    \item To assign severity levels to detected findings using documented security rules.
    \item To model identities, roles, permissions, and resources as a graph and visualize plausible access paths.
    \item To recommend security improvements that follow the principle of least privilege.
    \item To evaluate detection performance using a test dataset of secure and intentionally misconfigured policies.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Proposed Solution}

CloudGuard will provide a web interface through which users upload AWS policy JSON files or select predefined sample configurations. The backend will validate the input and analyze the policies with a rule-based security engine.

The system will identify suspicious permission patterns and display each finding with its severity, supporting evidence, potential impact, and recommended remediation. For configurations that contain sufficient identity, role, and resource relationships, CloudGuard will construct a graph of possible access paths so users can see how individual permissions and trust relationships combine.

The application will suggest improvements such as replacing unnecessary wildcard permissions with specific actions and resources, and restricting role assumption to trusted principals. The project will use simulated configurations and offline analysis. It will not execute attacks against real cloud infrastructure. Proposed configuration changes will be presented for review and will not be applied to a live AWS account.

\subsection{Key Features}

\subsubsection{IAM Policy Analyzer}

The IAM Policy Analyzer will parse uploaded JSON policy files and identify potentially excessive permissions, including unrestricted actions or resource access. Each finding will include an explanation of the relevant policy statement and a recommendation for reducing unnecessary permissions.

\subsubsection{Trust Policy Analyzer}

This component will examine IAM role trust policies for overly broad principals and potentially unsafe role-assumption conditions. Findings will depend on the available policy context and will distinguish suspicious configurations from confirmed access paths.

\subsubsection{S3 Bucket Policy Analyzer}

The S3 analyzer will examine sample bucket policies for statements that may permit public or otherwise unauthorized access to stored objects. It will explain the potential exposure and recommend access restrictions.

\subsubsection{Risk Scoring and Reporting}

The system will classify findings as Critical, High, Medium, or Low using predefined criteria. Users will be able to review findings, understand their potential impact, and obtain a summary of recommended corrective actions.

\subsubsection{Attack-Path Visualization}

CloudGuard will represent identities, roles, permissions, and resources as nodes and relationships in an interactive graph. Graph traversal will identify possible paths between modeled identities and sensitive resources. The analysis will use only relationships supported by the available configuration data. Assumptions and limitations will be documented, and a suspicious permission will not be treated as proof of an exploitable attack path.

\subsubsection{Remediation Recommendations}

For each finding, the application will suggest a safer configuration alternative. Suggestions may include restricting actions and resources, reviewing trust relationships, and removing unnecessary public access.

\subsection{Expected Outcomes}

The expected deliverables are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item A functional web application for analyzing selected AWS security policy configurations.
    \item A rule-based engine that detects the predefined cloud security misconfigurations.
    \item A dashboard that displays findings, severity levels, supporting evidence, and remediation recommendations.
    \item An interactive graph of modeled access relationships and plausible attack paths.
    \item A sample dataset of secure and intentionally misconfigured policy examples.
    \item A testing and evaluation report that documents detection performance, false positives, and known limitations.
    \item A final project report and a working demonstration.
\end{enumerate}

CloudGuard is intended as a practical and educational demonstration of information security principles applied to cloud access control. Users should be able to see which permissions may be risky and how combinations of permissions and trust relationships can create a broader security concern.

\section{Scope of the Project}

The project covers static analysis of selected AWS IAM identity policies, role trust policies, and S3 bucket policies. The delivered system will include risk classification, remediation guidance, and graph-based simulation from the relationships present in the supplied configurations.

The analysis is limited to the authorization relationships implemented in CloudGuard. Permissions boundaries, service control policies, session policies, additional resource-based policies, and explicit deny statements will be handled only to the extent supported by that analysis. Attack-path results will depend on how complete the input configuration is. A detected misconfiguration will be reported as a configuration weakness supported by the model. It will not be reported as proof of a successful attack. All demonstrations will use sample configurations in a controlled environment. Live AWS integration may be considered later only if time and resources permit.

\subsection{Proposed Technology Stack}

The following technologies are proposed:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Frontend:} Next.js, React, TypeScript, and Tailwind CSS.
    \item \textbf{Backend:} Node.js and Express.js for REST API endpoints.
    \item \textbf{Security analysis:} TypeScript-based policy parsing, validation, and custom security rules.
    \item \textbf{Graph analysis:} graph data structures with breadth-first search or depth-first search over modeled access relationships.
    \item \textbf{Graph visualization:} React Flow for identities, roles, resources, and possible access paths.
    \item \textbf{Database:} SQLite for scan history and findings, if persistent storage is required.
    \item \textbf{Testing:} Vitest and Supertest for security rules and backend API endpoints.
\end{itemize}

The security rules will be checked against AWS IAM and IAM Access Analyzer documentation, which are listed in the references.

\subsection{Testing and Evaluation}

CloudGuard will be tested with a predefined dataset of secure policies, intentionally misconfigured policies, and invalid JSON inputs. Evaluation will include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Verification of findings against manually reviewed expected results.
    \item Measurement of true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives.
    \item Calculation of precision and recall for the implemented detection rules.
    \item Verification that attack-path visualizations match the relationships present in the input data.
    \item Assessment of whether each remediation recommendation addresses the detected weakness.
\end{itemize}

\section{Initial Study and Work Done so Far}

The group has completed the study required to define this proposal. That study covered AWS IAM identity policies, IAM role trust policies, S3 bucket policies, the principle of least privilege, and common cloud misconfigurations described in AWS documentation. From this study, the group selected the weakness classes CloudGuard will detect: wildcard actions, wildcard resources, excessive privileges, overly broad trust principals, and bucket policies that may allow public access.

The group has also defined the system boundary, the severity categories, the graph model for access relationships, the technology stack, and the evaluation method based on precision and recall. Sample scenarios for secure and misconfigured policies have been specified in this proposal. The policy files, parser, rule engine, dashboard, attack-path view, and evaluation report are remaining implementation work. No live AWS account will be used for that work.

\subsection{Planned Methodology}

The remaining work will proceed in the following phases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dataset preparation:} Prepare sample secure and intentionally misconfigured policy files for the selected scenarios.
    \item \textbf{Policy parsing and validation:} Implement JSON validation and parsing so the system can process policy statements and reject malformed input.
    \item \textbf{Security rule development:} Develop a rule-based engine that detects the predefined weaknesses and produces evidence-based findings.
    \item \textbf{Risk assessment:} Assign severity from documented criteria, using permission scope, potential exposure, and the context available in the input.
    \item \textbf{Attack-path modeling:} Represent identities, roles, permissions, and resources as a graph, and use graph traversal to identify plausible paths supported by those relationships.
    \item \textbf{Frontend development:} Develop a dashboard for uploading policies, reviewing findings, exploring attack-path graphs, and reading remediation recommendations.
    \item \textbf{Testing and evaluation:} Compare system findings with manually reviewed expected results, and measure detection performance and false positives.
\end{enumerate}

